data.content:
## Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation Migration

### Problem

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it's completely removed. Your code uses three APIs that must be replaced:

| .NET Framework API | Status in .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Pattern Classification

Your code is a **cancellable work loop** with **ThreadAbortException as control flow** (rollback + reset abort to continue). The replacement is `CancellationToken` checked in the loop, with `OperationCanceledException` for cleanup.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort equivalent needed: just let the loop
                // condition re-evaluate. If you want to continue
                // processing remaining items, don't break here.
                // If you want a clean shutdown, break:
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signals cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot wake a blocked thread |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal — code checks at safe points |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed | Not needed — `OperationCanceledException` doesn't auto-rethrow like `ThreadAbortException` did |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` + abort | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Waits for natural exit instead of force-killing |
| — | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed to avoid timer/callback leaks |

### Critical Pitfall: `GetNextItem()` Must Accept the Token

The original `Thread.Abort()` could interrupt a thread blocked inside `GetNextItem()`. **Cooperative cancellation cannot do this.** If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a queue, socket, or `WaitHandle`, you must pass the token through:

```csharp
// If using BlockingCollection<T>:
var item = _queue.Take(token); // throws OCE when cancelled

// If using Thread.Sleep to poll:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval); // wakes on cancel

// If using synchronous I/O (e.g., socket):
// Switch to async, or use token.Register(() => socket.Close())
```

**If `GetNextItem()` calls code you can't modify** (third-party, native), cancellation will stall until that call returns. This is the biggest behavioral difference from `Thread.Abort`.

### Other Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Not passing token through the full call chain | `ProcessItem` should also accept `CancellationToken` and check it between expensive steps |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Implement `IDisposable` as shown — leaking it causes timer leaks |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between heavy operations inside `ProcessItem` |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Always use the `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |
